\section{Intersect Location Guarantees at a Join}

A reload justified on one incoming path may still read an
unrelated stack value on another. Complement the backward
view with a forward checker for one source temporary $t$.
On acyclic, corresponding source and target blocks, track
locations known to hold $t$ at each point. Assume a known
correspondence for the computation of $t$, independent
locations, no hidden aliases, and exact call clobber sets.
This toy check handles only the required returned value,
not correspondence of control flow or effects.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Acyclic corresponding blocks, initial location facts
\State Supply the entry facts on a synthetic incoming edge
\ForAll{blocks $B$ in topological order}
  \To{A guarantee must survive every incoming path.}
  \Let{K}{\operatorname{intersectionOfIncomingFacts}(B)}
  \ForAll{instructions $i$ of $B$}
    \Let{G}{\operatorname{equalitiesEstablished}(i,K)}
    \To{Compute new facts using pre-instruction locations.}
    \Let{K}{(K\setminus\operatorname{writtenLocations}(i))\cup G}
    \If{$i$ returns from a location outside $K$}
      \State \Return not certified
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State Record $K$ as the exit fact for $B$
\EndFor
\State \Return value obligation certified
\end{sudocode}

Let $t=23$ initially be in register $r$. The left arm copies
$r$ to private slot $s$ and then calls a function clobbering
$r$. The right arm merely calls that function. After both
arms, the left fact is $\{s\}$ and the right fact is empty.
A join that reloads $s$ and returns from $r$ cannot be
certified. With a concrete initial slot value of $5$, that
code returns $5$ on the right. Add the same spill to the
right arm before the call: the join now gets $\{s\}$ and
can reestablish $r=t$ by reload, returning $23$ on either path.

After each instruction every location in $K$ contains the
required source value on all paths to that point. Writing
a listed location kills the fact unless the transfer can
establish it again. Intersection can lose useful disjunctive
facts, so rejection may also mean the domain is too weak.
An implementation can keep this checker for small tests of a
backward validator and compare their answers on examples
with unambiguous obligations. With loops, a topological
walk must be replaced by an inductive annotation check or a
fixpoint computation.

\section{Compose Accepted Results, and Fail Safely}

A verified validator needs a theorem of the form $\mathrm{accept}(S,T)\Rightarrow T\preceq S$, where $\preceq$ is the chosen semantic relation. Use $T$ only on acceptance. For an optional same-IR optimization, failure can return $S$ unchanged. For necessary lowering, failure must either choose a separately justified translation or report a compilation failure. Hints from the optimizer, such as a block correspondence or a register assignment, can speed checking but must not bypass the validator's premises. To compose stages by behavior inclusion, use compatible observation semantics at their shared boundary and discharge the next stage's safety premise. A pass that only establishes agreement on terminating runs cannot be substituted for one that also establishes preservation of divergence.

The dossier's \emph{Transactional Compiler Transformations} offers a useful implementation pattern: hold a tentative IR revision privately, check the actual before-and-after pair and assumptions, and publish only the checked revision. Type and dominance checks are valuable but do not prove semantic preservation. The end-to-end claim must identify separately any stages outside the theorem, such as assembly, linking, loading, or foreign code; validating a local IR rewrite does not make those interfaces correct.
